window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][134] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should a subscription streaming service launch a cheaper, ad-supported tier?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, to reach more subscribers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs new subscribers against cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes revenue under cannibalization assumptions, finds the break-even number of new subscribers, considers ad load and retention, and designs a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of the decision and claim nothing about any real company's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;The question is whether the new tier adds more revenue than it takes from the premium tier. Suppose 1,000 premium subscribers at $15 a month: revenue $15,000. The ad tier is $8 plus about $5 a month of advertising revenue per subscriber, $13 in total. If 20% of premium subscribers move down and the tier adds 150 new subscribers, revenue is 800 × $15 + 200 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,550, up 10%. If 35% move down, it is 650 × $15 + 350 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,250. At 35% cannibalization, the tier needs at least 54 new subscribers to break even, about 5.4% growth. So the answer depends on the two uncertain numbers, the share who move down and the number who join. &quot;I'd also check ad revenue per subscriber, which depends on ad load and on advertisers, and retention: ad-tier subscribers may churn faster or slower; content costs are largely fixed, so the extra subscribers' margin is high. Fences matter: features the premium plan keeps (resolution, downloads, no ads) so that the move-down is limited. I'd test in one market with a holdout, measuring sign-ups, switching and retention at 90 days, with guardrails on satisfaction and ad complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Computed cannibalization and break-even; ad economics; fences; a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your referral program has a viral coefficient of 0.4. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Try to get it above 1 so it goes viral.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes what 0.4 means and what drives it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the multiplier and cycle effect, decomposes k into invitations and conversion, evaluates incentives against quality, and sets guardrails and unit economics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;At k = 0.4 each new user brings 0.4 more, so the loop is not self-sustaining, but it multiplies acquisition by 1 ÷ (1 − 0.4) = 1.67: 10,000 users acquired by other means become about 16,700 over several cycles, and the effective acquisition cost falls by 40%. That is worth having and worth improving; it is not a growth engine by itself. &quot;I'd decompose k: say 4 invitations per user at 10% conversion. Levers: invitations (make the prompt contextual, at the moment the user gets value, and give a reason to invite: sharing something the recipient will want); conversion (the invitation page and the first-use experience for the invited). Raising invitations to 5 and conversion to 12% gives k = 0.6 and a multiplier of 2.5. An incentive can raise both but may attract people who do not use the product, so I'd test it against a holdout and measure the retention and activation of referred users and the reward cost per retained user. &quot;Guardrails: spam complaints and unsubscribes, fraud (fake accounts to earn rewards), and the quality of referred users compared with other channels. Unit economics: if a reward costs $5 and a referred user is worth $30 in contribution, it pays; if referred users churn twice as fast, it may not.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Multiplier; decomposition; incentive tradeoffs; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you launch a significant new feature to existing users? Give the plan and the success criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Announce it by email and in the app.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A staged launch with a checklist and metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets a segment with a message, a launch tier and timeline, a readiness checklist across functions, success criteria with guardrails and rollback triggers, a holdout, and a post-launch review.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First, who it is for within the base and what problem it solves for them: say a scheduling feature for the 30% of users who book appointments weekly. The message: 'book in two taps instead of six,' with proof from the beta. The tier: a staged rollout with an announcement only after the first stage is stable (C17). &quot;Timeline. Eight weeks out: positioning, success metrics and rollout plan agreed. Six weeks: beta with 2,000 users; feedback. Four weeks: documentation, support and sales enablement, in-product messages drafted. Two weeks: a readiness review across product, engineering, design, data, support, legal and marketing, with a checklist (quality, performance, accessibility, privacy, instrumentation, help content, rollback). Launch week: 5%, then 25%, then 100% over a week, with a daily check. Two to four weeks after: a review. &quot;Success criteria, written in advance: the share of target users who use the feature at least twice in the first two weeks (primary; target 35%), repeat usage at 30 days, and impact on the core metric (bookings per user) against a 5% holdout; guardrails: support contacts per user, crash rate, and cancellations; rollback if crash rate doubles or bookings per user fall more than 2%. I'd review adoption, retention and the holdout comparison, and decide to expand, iterate or roll back.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A target and message; a staged plan; cross-functional readiness; criteria and rollback; a holdout.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customer acquisition cost has risen 40% over two quarters. Diagnose it and decide what to do.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Cut marketing spend.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by channel and checks conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the effect on payback and LTV:CAC, decomposes the rise (mix, saturation, conversion, price or product changes), checks cohort quality, and decides with a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First the effect: CAC rose from $45 to $63. With contribution of $6 a month (ARPU $8, margin 75%), payback went from 7.5 to 10.5 months, and with LTV of $120 the ratio fell from 2.67 to 1.90, below the usual threshold of about 3 <code>[V: arithmetic checked; the 3:1 and 12-month benchmarks are common heuristics, not sourced]</code>. So it matters. &quot;Diagnose by decomposing CAC into spend and new customers, by channel: is spend up while volume is flat (saturation: the cheapest audiences are used up and we are buying more expensive ones), did the mix shift toward a costly channel, did conversion fall (a landing page or product change; the funnel step where it fell), did a competitor or an auction raise prices, or is attribution broken (a tracking change counts fewer customers)? I'd also check the quality: do new customers retain as well as before? A cheap but poor channel and an expensive but good one look different in CAC and the same in value. &quot;Then decide with a rule: for each channel compute LTV:CAC and payback; cut or cap channels below the threshold; shift spend to channels that pass; fix the funnel step that fell if that is the cause; and improve retention if LTV is the lever. I would not cut across the board: it may drop the good channels with the bad.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The effect on payback and ratio; decomposition; cohort quality; a channel-level rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What happens when you open an app and your feed loads?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The app asks the server for the data and shows it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Walks the path: device, name lookup, connection, server, database, response, rendering.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Says where time and failure concentrate, mentions caching and percentiles, and ties the path to what the user sees when a hop fails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll walk the path in the order it happens, and say where it can go wrong. First, the app on my phone decides what to ask for: the latest items for my account. It needs the address of the company's server, so it asks the Domain Name System, which turns a name into a numeric address. A copy of recent answers is kept on the phone or the network, so this is usually fast. Then the phone opens a secure connection to the server's front door, which is usually a load balancer that spreads requests among many servers, often behind a content delivery network that already holds common files near me. The request reaches an application server, which checks who I am, then asks other services and a database for what goes into my feed: my follows, recent posts, ranking, maybe ads. It may use a cache for popular items. It assembles a response and sends it back, and my phone draws it, loading images as I scroll. <em>Where time goes.</em> On a good network, the server and database work dominates; on a poor network, connection setup and transfer dominate; on an old phone, drawing can. So when someone says it is slow I ask which hop and at which percentile, because the median can look fine while one user in twenty waits several seconds. <em>Where it fails, and what the user sees.</em> If the name lookup fails, nothing loads. If the database is slow, the feed is slow. If one service (say ads) is down, a well-designed app shows the feed without ads and not a blank screen: graceful degradation. The thing I would check is what each hop shows the user when it fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain to a non-technical stakeholder what an API is.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's how programs talk to each other.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain definition, an analogy and a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds the contract idea, versioning, and why it matters to the business (partners, speed, risk), and says one tradeoff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> An API is a menu of requests that one piece of software offers to another. Like a restaurant menu, it says what you can ask for, in what form, and what you will get back, without you needing to know how the kitchen works. An example from a product: when a travel app shows the weather, it does not measure the weather. It sends a request to a weather company's API, &quot;forecast for this city tomorrow,&quot; and gets back a small, structured answer that the app displays. When you pay in an online shop, the shop does not handle your card; it calls a payment company's API, &quot;charge this amount,&quot; and gets back &quot;approved&quot; or &quot;declined.&quot; Why this matters for the business. First, it lets us build on others' work and lets others build on ours, which is how platforms grow. Second, an API is a <em>contract</em>: once partners build on it, we cannot change it casually, so changing it is a planned, announced process with versions. Third, it is a place where reliability and security are decided: every request is a door, and each needs a lock, a limit on how often it can be used, and monitoring. One tradeoff to know: making an API simple for partners means committing to keep it working, which limits how freely our own engineers can reshape the system behind it. So I ask what the contract is, who depends on it, and what it costs to change.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain on-device versus cloud processing for a voice assistant, including privacy and latency.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;On-device is more private and cloud is more powerful.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares latency, privacy, capability, cost and offline use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies latency on good and poor networks, proposes a hybrid with routing and disclosure, and treats sensitive requests specially.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real system. <em>On-device processing</em> means the phone does the work itself; <em>cloud processing</em> means it sends the audio or its transcript to a server and gets the answer back. <em>Privacy.</em> On the device, the data never leaves, which is the strongest guarantee and a trust signal. In the cloud, the data is sent and handled by a server, so the company must justify what it collects, how long it keeps it, and who can see it. Users should be told which happens and given control. <em>Latency.</em> The device has no network wait, but its processor is slower than a server's. Suppose (invented) on-device compute takes 150 ms; the cloud computes in 40 ms but needs a round trip of 60 ms and 20 ms to upload on a good connection, 120 ms in all; on a poor connection with a 400 ms round trip it is 460 ms, and offline it fails. So the cloud is slightly faster when the network is good and much slower when it is not; on-device is predictable. <em>Capability.</em> The cloud can run larger models and use more data, so it handles hard requests better; the device is limited by memory and battery. Suppose easy requests (80% of traffic) are handled at about 95% accuracy on the device and hard ones at 70%, against 90% for hard requests in the cloud. <em>Cost.</em> The cloud costs the company per request (say $0.002), which at a billion requests a day is $2 million a day; the device shifts cost to the user's battery and the model's size on the phone. <em>My recommendation.</em> A hybrid: do the easy 80% on the device and send only the hard 20% to the cloud, with a short routing step. In my numbers that gives an average latency near 146 ms, accuracy near 94% (against 90% for device only and about 95% for cloud only), and a fifth of the cloud cost. I would keep sensitive categories on the device whatever their difficulty, tell users plainly what is sent, and measure the share routed to the cloud. What would change my view: on-device quality closing the gap, which would move more work to the device. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that technical-fluency prompts at Apple concern on-device processing and privacy <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product's design, numbers or data handling.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customers say a page is slow. How do you work with engineers to find out why and what to do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask engineering to make it faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for measurements by hop and percentile, finds the biggest contributor, and decides on a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses a latency budget, checks the slow devices and networks, considers side effects and measures the effect on users.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd turn &quot;slow&quot; into numbers before asking for any fix. I'd ask engineering for the load time at the median and the 95th percentile, split by hop: name lookup and connection, server compute, database, transfer, and rendering. I'd also ask who is slow: which devices, networks and countries, because averages hide the slow minority, and the customers who complain are often in it. Then I'd find the largest contributor at the percentile that matters. Suppose the tail is 5.2 seconds and 2.0 of it is one database query. By Amdahl's law, speeding up a hop that is 5% of the time can save at most 5%, so I would not start with the front-end polish. I'd ask the engineer what is slow about that query; a common answer is a missing index, a cheap fix. I'd also ask about image sizes (transfer) and whether any result could be cached and how stale it may be. I'd put a target in writing: a 95th-percentile load time under 3 seconds on a mid-range phone on a typical mobile connection, not &quot;faster.&quot; I'd ask what the fixes cost, what they risk (a cache can show stale data, an index slows writes slightly), and how we will roll them out with a flag and a holdback so that we can measure the effect on bounce and conversion, not just on load time. Finally I'd set a guardrail so that speed does not come at the cost of correctness, and I'd re-measure after each change. What I would not do: assign a rewrite without knowing where the time goes.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Users say that after saving a change to their profile, they sometimes see the old version. What could cause this, and what would you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's a bug; ask engineering to fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names caching and copies of data as causes, and explains staleness.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes causes (browser or app cache, server cache, read replica lag), sizes the problem, proposes read-your-own-writes and design options with tradeoffs, and sets a measure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is a classic symptom of data that exists in more than one place, so one copy is updated before the others. I'd list where copies live, because the cause depends on which one is stale. <em>Possible causes.</em> (1) <em>The client's cache:</em> the app or browser keeps the old profile and shows it until it refreshes. (2) <em>A server-side cache:</em> the application stores a copy of the profile for speed, and the write did not clear it, or the cache's lifetime has not expired. (3) <em>Read replicas:</em> to handle load, the database keeps copies that serve reads, and a copy can lag behind the main one by a short time (eventual consistency); if the page after saving reads from a lagging copy, the user sees the old data. (4) <em>A queue:</em> if the write is processed asynchronously, the change may not have been applied yet. <em>Size it.</em> Ask for the rate and timing. Suppose (invented) 200,000 profile saves a day, and 5% of users view their profile within two seconds of saving, which is about 10,000 users a day who could see stale data if replica lag is about two seconds. That is a visible, trust-damaging number: people think their change was lost, and some will save again or contact support. <em>Options.</em> (A) <em>Read-your-own-writes:</em> for a few seconds after a user writes, route their reads to the main database or the fresh cache. Cost: a small extra load on the main database (here 10,000 reads a day, negligible) and a little logic. (B) <em>Show the new data immediately on the client,</em> then reconcile (optimistic update). Cost: a brief inconsistency if the save fails, which needs a clear error. (C) <em>Shorten cache lifetimes or clear the cache on write.</em> Cost: less speed benefit. (D) <em>Accept it:</em> acceptable for data like a follower count, not for something the user just changed. <em>Decision.</em> A and B together for user-initiated changes (the user must see their own change at once), and keep the long cache lifetimes for other users' views of the profile, where a delay of seconds is harmless. Measure: the rate of &quot;I saved but see the old value&quot; support contacts and a client check of whether the displayed value matches the saved one. The lesson to say aloud: speed comes from copies, and copies can disagree, so the product decides where staleness is acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app wants to set reliability targets. How would you approach it, and what would you commit to for sending messages versus loading media?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;As reliable as possible, five nines.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets different targets for different functions, using the cost of failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes downtime for each target, compounds dependencies, defines an SLO and error budget with a policy, and designs graceful degradation and retries with idempotency.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd begin with what a failure costs the user, because a single target for everything wastes effort. Sending a message is the core promise; if it fails, people lose trust fast. Loading a photo that arrives a moment late is an annoyance. <em>Targets and what they mean.</em> A month has about 43,834 minutes. A target of 99.95% for sending allows 0.0005 × 43,834 = about 22 minutes of failure a month. A target of 99.5% for loading media allows 219 minutes, about 3.6 hours. I would not say &quot;five nines&quot; without a price: each extra nine costs more redundancy and complexity, so I'd ask engineering what 99.95% costs against 99.9% (44 minutes) and compare it with what an extra 22 minutes of failures cost in trust. <em>Dependencies.</em> Sending depends on several services (authentication, the message store, the delivery service); in a chain, availability multiplies. Three services at 99.95%, 99.95% and 99.9% give about 99.8%, which is 88 minutes of failure, worse than the target. So either the chain must be shortened (can delivery be asynchronous?) or each part must be better. <em>Design for the failure.</em> Make sending resilient: save the message on the device, show it as &quot;sending,&quot; and retry in the background until the server confirms, with an idempotency key so that a retry never produces a duplicate message. For media, load a low-resolution preview first and fetch the full file when possible, so a slow media service degrades the experience without breaking the conversation. <em>SLO and error budget.</em> Define the objective in terms users feel: 99.95% of send attempts are accepted within two seconds, measured monthly. The budget is about 22 minutes. A policy decided in advance: if less than a quarter of the budget remains, pause risky launches and focus on reliability; if the budget is spent, only fixes ship until it recovers. <em>Measures.</em> Successful sends per attempt, the 95th and 99th percentile send time, duplicate message rate (should be zero), and how long messages sit in &quot;sending.&quot; What would change my approach: evidence that outages in one region hurt a particular market much more, which would justify regional redundancy there.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Engineering says a change requires a database migration with two hours of downtime. How do you evaluate it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it at night and tell customers,&quot; or &quot;downtime is unacceptable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the cost of downtime against the cost of avoiding it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Asks the ten questions, computes cost and the effect on the reliability budget and contracts, knows the expand-and-contract alternative, and plans rollback and communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a cost and risk decision and start with questions to engineering (FW-C21-02): why is downtime needed, what changes in the data, what is the riskiest step, and what is the rollback if it fails halfway? Is there a way to migrate without downtime? <em>Cost of the downtime.</em> Suppose (invented) the product earns $20,000 an hour at peak. A two-hour window at 03:00, when traffic is about 15% of peak, costs about 2 × $20,000 × 0.15 = $6,000 in lost sales, plus support contacts and some trust. That is small. <em>Cost of avoiding it.</em> The alternative is a staged &quot;expand and contract&quot; migration: add the new structure beside the old, write to both, copy the old data in the background, switch reads to the new, then remove the old. It avoids downtime and adds a few weeks; suppose three person-weeks at $15,000 each, $45,000, plus more moving parts and more risk of a subtle bug during the dual-write period. <em>Reliability budget and contracts.</em> At a 99.9% monthly objective the budget is 43.8 minutes; two hours is 120 minutes, about 2.7 times the budget. If customers have service agreements, check whether planned maintenance is excluded and how much notice is required; some enterprise contracts forbid planned downtime in business hours or require notice. So the cash comparison ($6,000 against $45,000) is not the whole story: the budget and contracts may force the staged approach. <em>Plan either way.</em> Rehearse the migration on a copy of production data and time it (two hours is an estimate, not a measurement). Set a go or no-go point and a rollback that is tested. Take a verified backup. Choose the quietest window and tell customers in advance with a status page. Have support ready. Define a trigger for abort (for example, if the copy is not 60% complete by the midpoint). <em>Decision.</em> If the contracts allow notice and the window is quiet, accept the planned two hours, with the rehearsal and rollback plan. If contracts or the budget forbid it, or if the data is critical and rollback is hard, fund the staged migration. What would change my mind: a rehearsal showing the migration takes six hours, which would make the staged route clearly better.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What does a PM need to show in a system design question, and how do you start one?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd name the technologies I'd use.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies requirements, estimates scale, sketches the flow and covers failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the PM-level bar, states assumptions and the decisions the numbers drive, covers abuse and privacy, and closes with an executive summary and questions for engineering.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The question is not whether I can build the system; it is whether I can turn an open request into a bounded problem, size it, sketch how the pieces fit, weigh tradeoffs, and say what breaks. So I start with questions, not boxes. I begin by saying my plan: &quot;I'll clarify who it's for and what it must do, estimate the scale, sketch the main flows, go deep on the part that matters most, then cover failure and abuse, and summarize.&quot; Then I ask the few questions whose answers change the design: who the users are and the core job; the scale (users and actions a day); how fast and how fresh results must be; what must never be lost or duplicated; what the privacy or regulatory constraints are; and what is out of scope. Next I estimate: daily users times actions, divided by 86,400 seconds, gives an average rate; a peak factor gives the peak. I say what decision each number drives. Then I draw a handful of boxes (clients, entry point, a few services, data stores, a queue) and walk one write and one read through them, saying where the user waits. I name the main tradeoff and choose with the user in mind, say what the user sees when something fails, and run a quick abuse pass if the system touches people or money. I finish with a 60-second summary for an executive and the one thing I would validate with engineering first.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the traffic, storage and bandwidth for a photo-sharing app with 10 million daily users.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> One large number with no method.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses users × actions ÷ 86,400, a peak factor, item sizes and retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States each assumption, separates reads and writes, uses the numbers to draw conclusions (CDN, storage cost), and sanity-checks.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll state assumptions and use the sheet. Suppose each daily user uploads 1.5 photos and views 40. <em>Uploads.</em> 10,000,000 × 1.5 = 15,000,000 photos a day, 15,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = about 174 a second; at a peak factor of 4, about 700 a second. That is a low write rate: ordinary for a queue-based design. <em>Storage.</em> Suppose the original averages 2.5 MB and resized versions add about 0.3 MB: 2.8 MB a photo. 15,000,000 × 2.8 MB = 42 TB a day, about 15 PB a year. Storage is the big number: it will dominate cost, so I would ask about compression, a retention policy, and cheaper storage for old photos. <em>Views.</em> 10,000,000 × 40 = 400,000,000 views a day, about 4,630 a second, and 18,500 at the peak factor of 4. <em>Bandwidth.</em> If a viewed photo averages 200 KB, 4,630 views a second is about 925 MB a second, about 7.4 Gbps on average and about 30 Gbps at peak. <em>What the numbers say.</em> Writes are modest; reads and bandwidth are large, so serve photos from a content delivery network near users, with a cache for hot items. Storage grows by about 42 TB a day, so cost per user matters: 42 TB a day over 10 million users is 4.2 MB per user per day. Sanity check: 15 PB a year is large but plausible for a large photo product.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a shared photo album service for families.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Upload photos and share a link.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Covers permissions, storage ownership, notifications, and scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Raises link-sharing risk and revocation, deletion semantics, abuse, cost per user, and degradation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real design. The job: family members add photos to an album and see everyone's photos, with notifications when something is new. <em>Requirements.</em> Create an album, invite people, add and view photos, comment, get notified. Privacy: only invited people can see it, with the option of a link for relatives who do not have accounts. Durability: photos must not be lost. Freshness: new photos appear within seconds. Non-goals: editing tools. <em>Scale (invented).</em> 20 million daily users; 5 million photos a day added to shared albums, each notifying 4 other members: 20 million notifications a day, about 230 a second; 30 million album opens a day, about 350 a second. Modest rates; storage and sharing rules are the design issues. <em>Sketch.</em> An album is a record with a member list and permissions; photos go to object storage with thumbnails made by a worker; a &quot;new photo&quot; event goes on a queue; a notification service sends a push to members who opted in, batching bursts (&quot;Ana added 12 photos&quot;). Album opens read the member list, check permission, and serve thumbnails from a content network. <em>Decisions that matter.</em> <em>Who owns the storage:</em> if a photo counts against the uploader's quota, uploading to a shared album has a cost to that person, so say so plainly. <em>Link sharing:</em> an unlisted link is convenient and can leak, so use long random links, let owners turn them off, and show who has viewed. <em>Deletion:</em> if the owner leaves or deletes the account, decide whether the album persists for members; I'd transfer ownership and warn first. <em>Removal:</em> when someone leaves, they lose access to the album but keep the photos they uploaded unless they delete them. <em>Failure and abuse.</em> If thumbnails fail, show a placeholder and retry. If notifications are delayed, the album still shows new photos. Abuse: an unwanted person added to an album (limit invites, allow a recipient to decline and report), explicit content shared (reporting and scanning, with care for privacy). <em>Measures.</em> Albums with two or more contributors, weekly photos added per album, notification opt-out rate, link-leak reports. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of sharing and photo design prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design &quot;Stories&quot;: photos and videos that disappear after 24 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Store the posts and delete after 24 hours.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Handles expiry, views, fan-out, and storage.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates read-time hiding from deletion, covers the view list and privacy, estimates load, and covers abuse and ephemerality promises.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real design. The job: people post short photos and videos that friends can view for 24 hours. <em>Requirements.</em> Post a story; friends see it in a tray; the poster sees who viewed it; it disappears after 24 hours. Freshness: a new story appears in seconds. Privacy: only the audience the poster chose. Non-goals: permanent archives in version 1. <em>Scale (invented).</em> 100 million daily users; 15% post a story a day with an average of 3 items: 45 million items a day, about 520 a second. Each item is viewed by about 40 people: 1.8 billion views a day, about 20,800 a second on average. A video averages 3 MB: 45 million × 3 MB = 135 TB a day of media. <em>Sketch.</em> <em>Write:</em> upload media to object storage, create a story record (poster, audience, expiry time = posted time + 24 hours), publish an event. <em>Tray:</em> for each viewer, the set of friends with unexpired stories; because stories are few and short-lived, fan-out on read is workable: when a user opens the app, fetch the friends' latest stories, with a cache of &quot;who has an active story.&quot; <em>View:</em> a view writes a record (viewer, story, time), appended through a queue and counted; the poster's view list reads from it. <em>Expiry.</em> Hide at read time: every read checks that the expiry time has not passed, so a story disappears exactly at 24 hours regardless of cleanup. Delete the media and records later with a background job within a set period, which keeps storage bounded: media is held about 24 hours plus a grace period, so the live store holds about 135 TB, not a growing archive. Whether the platform keeps an archive for the poster or for safety review is a policy decision with privacy consequences, and should be stated. <em>Tradeoffs.</em> View counts: exact counts cost writes; approximate counts that settle in seconds are fine, but the list of viewers should be accurate (people care who saw it). Consistency: eventual is fine for the tray; permissions must be strict at read time. <em>Failure and abuse.</em> If the view-recording queue backs up, counts lag and are labeled &quot;updating.&quot; Abuse: screenshots cannot be prevented, so promise only what the product can keep (&quot;disappears from the app,&quot; not &quot;can't be saved&quot;); harassment through stories needs reporting, blocking and review that works within the 24-hour window. <em>Measures.</em> Stories posted per user, viewing completion, time to appear (95th percentile under 5 seconds), reports per 1,000 stories handled within hours. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of ephemeral-content design prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> system design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C22 · <strong>Competency:</strong> SD · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design real-time co-editing for a shared document in an enterprise product.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Save the document on a server and let people open it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Covers presence, conflict handling, latency, offline, and permissions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains conflict resolution choices in plain terms, protects against lost text, covers audit and compliance, and states what to ask engineering.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real design. The job: several people edit the same document at once and see each other's changes quickly, without losing work. <em>Requirements.</em> Up to about 10 simultaneous editors; remote changes appear within about 200 ms; no lost text; works through brief connection loss; permissions (view, comment, edit) enforced; an audit trail for compliance; version history. Non-goals: editing the same cell of a table with perfect merging in version 1. <em>The central problem: conflicts.</em> Two people type in the same sentence at the same moment. The system must decide how to combine edits. Options in plain terms: <em>last writer wins</em> (simple, loses someone's change), <em>locking</em> (only one editor per section, safe, frustrating), or <em>merging edits as operations</em> (each change is recorded as an operation such as &quot;insert 'the' at position 12&quot;, and a rule transforms concurrent operations so that everyone's document converges to the same result). Engineers use families of techniques for the third; I would not specify which, only the requirement: all copies converge and no one's typing disappears. <em>Sketch.</em> Each editor's client sends operations to a collaboration service for that document, which orders them, applies them, and broadcasts them to the other editors; presence (cursors, who is online) travels on the same channel but is not stored. Operations are stored in a log, which gives version history and the audit trail. <em>Scale (invented).</em> 10 active editors typing about 5 operations a second each: 50 operations a second per document. A document session is small; the scale comes from millions of documents, so route each document's session to one server and spread documents across servers. <em>Offline.</em> Edits made offline are kept on the device and merged on reconnect. If the same sentence was changed on both sides, the system keeps both versions of the conflicting passage and flags it for a person to resolve, rather than guess. Suppose 2% of offline sessions have such a conflict (to be measured). <em>Permissions, audit, compliance.</em> Permissions are checked on every operation, not just at open. The log records who changed what and when, retained per the customer's policy, with the ability to export for audit. Data residency: keep a customer's documents in their region. <em>Failure.</em> If the collaboration service fails, clients keep working locally and reconnect; show &quot;reconnecting&quot; and never discard unsent edits. A server restart replays the log. <em>Questions for engineering.</em> How do we guarantee convergence, what is our plan if one document has 200 editors, and how do we test conflict cases? <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of collaboration and enterprise design prompts <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or design is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-06</h3>\n";
